# Target C-II — `t<=1.8436` forces macroscopic O--O families in both leaf phases

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only near-frontier phase localization  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** `g=0.0077` residual-coupled floor/stability; phase-resolved retained outer mass for `t<=1.844`; branch-aware physical center/leaf pair theorem; dominant-orbit triangle-free correlation  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

Write the dominant exact `pi/3` orbit phase masses as

\[
C\ge U\ge V\ge0.
\]

The `t<=1.844` phase theorem already gives retained outer-occurrence masses

\[
z_C,z_U,z_V>0.616-o(1).
\]

Previously only the smallest leaf was known to have train mass below `0.616D`, so only
that phase was forced to contain a linear retained O--O family.  Very near the current
scalar frontier the residual-coupled theorem is much more rigid.

This note proves that every survivor with

\[
\boxed{t=(q-k)/D\le1.8436}
\]

satisfies

\[
\boxed{U<0.513-o(1),\qquad V<0.513-o(1),}
\tag{TL.1}
\]

and hence both leaf phases carry retained O--O pair weight

\[
\boxed{P_U>0.103-o(1),\qquad P_V>0.103-o(1).}
\tag{TL.2}
\]

Thus the live frontier contains two macroscopic O--O families whose exact projective
phases differ by `pi/3`.

---

## 1. Residual slack is below `1.3e-4`

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
C_{77}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0077,
\]

and use the residual-coupled variables

\[
U_{net}={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\kappa/2,
\qquad
h=U_{net}-C_{77},
\qquad
x={\kappa\over2}.
\]

The `g=.0077` residual theorem gives exact-orbit residual mass

\[
R:=U_{net}-M\le1.05h,
\tag{TL.3}
\]

where `M=C+U+V` is the dominant orbit mass.  Its survivor-capacity function decreases
strictly in both `h` and `x` throughout the present box.

At

\[
t=1.8436,\qquad h=0.00013,\qquad x=0,
\]

the exact certificate evaluates the equivalent direct condition and proves the strict
contradiction margin

\[
F(A)-\delta>4.60\times10^{-6},
\]

where

\[
F(s)=s-6\sin(s/6),
\]

\[
\delta={1\over2}
\left({9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{77}-h\right),
\]

and

\[
A=\pi-{t\over2}-1.05h
-{1.05^2h^2\over2(1-\delta)}.
\]

Since survivor capacity increases with `t` and decreases with `h,x`, every survivor with
`t<=1.8436` must satisfy

\[
\boxed{h<0.00013-o(1).}
\tag{TL.4}
\]

Consequently

\[
M\le U_{net}<C_{77}+0.00013<1.912456+o(1),
\tag{TL.5}
\]

and (TL.3) gives

\[
R<1.05(0.00013)+o(1)=0.0001365+o(1).
\]

All non-dominant exact orbits together carry crossing mass at most `R^2/4`.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{E_{dom}>0.90689967-o(1).}
\tag{TL.6}
\]

The exact verifier checks this directly from the Machin/radical enclosure for
`Delta2`.

---

## 2. A preliminary lower bound on the smallest leaf

The existing phase theorem gives

\[
z_V>0.616-o(1).
\]

Every complete train has only two terminal occurrences, each retained with weight at
most one.  Hence

\[
z_V\le2V+o(1),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{V>0.308-o(1).}
\tag{TL.7}

This lower bound is used only to keep the phase split inside the strong physical pair
branch below.

---

## 3. Phase-resolved crossing capacity

Let

\[
x_{CU}=CU-e(C,U),
\qquad
y_{CV}=CV-e(C,V)
\]

be the missing center/leaf crossing masses.  The hostile-audited triangle-free
correlation gives

\[
\left(1-{V\over C}\right)x_{CU}
+
\left(1-{U\over C}\right)y_{CV}
\le C(U+V)-E_{dom}.
\tag{TL.8}
\]

For the present near-frontier box, the strong branch of the physical pair theorem is
valid for both center/leaf pairs.  Thus

\[
x_{CU}\ge{1\over10}(C+U-K)^2,
\qquad
y_{CV}\ge{1\over10}(C+V-K)^2,
\qquad K={2\over\sqrt3},
\tag{TL.9}
\]

throughout the only potentially feasible region.  Substituting (TL.9) into (TL.8)
gives

\[
\boxed{
E_{dom}
\le
C(U+V)
-{1\over10}\left(1-{V\over C}\right)(C+U-K)^2
-{1\over10}\left(1-{U\over C}\right)(C+V-K)^2.
}
\tag{TL.10}
\]

The positive parts may be inserted in (TL.9); in the compact region used by the
certificate both excesses are already positive.

---

## 4. Suppose the larger leaf has mass at least `0.513`

Assume for contradiction

\[
U\ge U_0:=0.513.
\]

Put the safe total-mass upper bound

\[
W_0:=1.912456.
\]

For fixed `C,W`, write `V=W-C-U`.  The right side of (TL.10) has two useful
monotonicities while `C>=U>=V`:

1. it increases with `W`;
2. it decreases as the larger leaf mass `U` increases at fixed `C,W`.

For the second statement, if

\[
X={1\over10}(C+U-K)^2,
\qquad
Y={1\over10}(C+V-K)^2,
\]

then `U>=V` and `C<K` give `X>=Y` and

\[
1-{V\over C}\ge1-{U\over C}.
\]

Direct differentiation therefore makes both differences nonpositive.  The `W`
derivative is positive because its leading term is `C`, while the physical-deficit
correction is below `0.1` on the certified box.

There are two `C` regimes.

### Low center: `C<=W_0-2U_0=0.886456`

Here the condition `U>=0.513` imposes no stronger split restriction than the balanced
leaf endpoint.  The strong aggregate phase-star capacity applies.  It is increasing on
this interval, and at its right endpoint the exact verifier gives

\[
\Psi_{strong}(W_0,0.886456)<E_0-0.00244,
\]

where

\[
E_0:=0.90689967.
\]

This contradicts (TL.6).

### Main interval: `0.886456<=C<1.044`

Generic center capacity already excludes `C>=1.044`, since

\[
1.044(1.912456-1.044)<0.90689967.
\]

On the remaining interval, replacing `W` by `W_0` and `U` by `U_0` can only increase
the right side of (TL.10).  Then

\[
V=W_0-C-U_0\le U_0.
\]

The worst center/leaf strong-branch ratio is below

\[
0.78421<\sqrt{20/29},
\]

so the `1/10` pair theorem is valid for both leaves on the entire interval.

Define the resulting one-variable upper capacity by

\[
\begin{aligned}
G(C)=&\ C(W_0-C)\\
&-{1\over10}\left(1-{W_0-C-U_0\over C}\right)(C+U_0-K)^2\\
&-{1\over10}\left(1-{U_0\over C}\right)(W_0-U_0-K)^2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{TL.11}
\]

The exact derivative calculation gives

\[
G''(C)<-2
\]

throughout `[0.886456,1.044]`.  At

\[
c_0=0.933067,
\]

the tangent bound for a function with second derivative at most `-2` gives

\[
\max_CG(C)
\le G(c_0)+{G'(c_0)^2\over4}.
\]

The exact rational certificate obtains

\[
\boxed{
\max_CG(C)<0.906892586
<0.90689967-7.08\times10^{-6}.
}
\tag{TL.12}
\]

Again this contradicts (TL.6).  Therefore the assumption `U>=0.513` is impossible,
proving (TL.1).

---

## 5. Both leaf phases are outer-to-outer at linear scale

For one exact phase class `i`, let `P_i` be its retained O--O pair weight.  If a train
has terminal retained weights `a_tau,b_tau`, then

\[
a_\tau+b_\tau\le1+\min(a_\tau,b_\tau).
\]

Summing within phase `i` gives

\[
z_i\le M_i+P_i+o(1),
\tag{TL.13}
\]

where `M_i` is that phase's train mass.

Apply this to the two leaves.  The existing phasewise theorem gives

\[
z_U,z_V>0.616-o(1),
\]

while (TL.1) gives `U,V<0.513-o(1)`.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
P_U>0.103-o(1),
\qquad
P_V>0.103-o(1).
}
\]

Each family is pairwise noncrossing internally because it lies in one exact phase, and
every member is two-sidedly diameter-braced by Section 157.

The two phase directions differ by exactly `pi/3`.  Therefore the existing low-energy
cross-phase O--O theorem now applies to **two macroscopic pair populations**, rather
than one leaf population against an unknown second phase.

---

